== Record claims ==

In August 1993, McLaren tested the XP3 prototype – which was limited to {{convert|433|kW|hp PS|abbr=on|0}} – at the [[Nardò Ring]]. They calculated a top speed of {{convert|231|mph|abbr=on}} from the data recording inside the car.<ref name="MF1book">{{cite web |url=https://issuu.com/themagazineshop/docs/mclaren_f1 |title=Autocar Book – McLaren F1 |date=12 November 2013 |access-date=19 August 2016}}</ref>

The British magazine [[Autocar (magazine)|''Autocar'']] was given access and tested the XP5 prototype in May 1994. They wrote: "Had we enough tarmac, we have no doubt that it would finally stop accelerating at its rev-limiter in top which, taking tyre growth into account, would be somewhere the far side of {{convert|230|mph|abbr=on}}."<ref name="AC"/>

''[[Car and Driver]]'' wrote in their August 1994 issue ("Courtesy of Autocar & Motor" written in the box with performance numbers):
"Top speed? The F1 runs into the 7,500 rpm redline in sixth at {{convert|221|mph|abbr=on}} but it's still accelerating. Gordon Murray, the F1's designer, is convinced that with taller gearing, the car is capable of at least {{convert|230|mph|abbr=on}}."<ref name="CaD"/>

On 31 March 1998, [[Andy Wallace (racing driver)|Andy Wallace]] drove the five-year-old XP5 prototype at [[Volkswagen]]'s test track in [[Ehra-Lessien]], setting a new production car world record. The record consisted of an independently measured {{convert|240.1|mi/h|km/h|abbr=on}} two-way average with the peak speed of {{convert|243|mph|km/h|abbr=on}} measured by McLaren<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wYmIfDAQ9Y0  |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/varchive/youtube/20211212/wYmIfDAQ9Y0| archive-date=2021-12-12 |url-status=live|title=McLaren F1 243mph run |date=18 March 2011 |publisher=YouTube |access-date=19 August 2016}}{{cbignore}}</ref> with the rev-limiter raised to 8,300 rpm.<ref name="limiter">{{cite web |url=http://www.mclaren.com/mclaren/features/limiter.html |title=Many superlatives have described the McLaren F1, including reference to its prodigious speed. We all know it's quick, but now it's officially quick... |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20010209004913/http://www.mclaren.com/mclaren/features/limiter.html |archive-date=9 February 2001 |access-date=19 August 2016}}</ref>

As of 2022, the F1 remains the fastest naturally aspirated production car in the world, a record that has been held for 24 years. Although its speed record has been passed, the cars that have surpassed it use forced induction engines.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.roadandtrack.com/car-culture/classic-cars/videos/a32423/mclaren-f1-top-speed-record-history/|title=How the 240-MPH McLaren F1 Nonchalantly Became the World's Fastest Car|date=26 January 2017|first=Chris|last=Perkins|publisher=[[Road & Track]]|access-date=17 February 2020}}</ref>

== Motorsports ==
{{Main|McLaren F1 GTR}}
Following its initial launch as a road car, motorsports teams convinced McLaren to build racing versions of the F1 to compete in international series. Three different versions of the race car were developed from 1995 to 1997.<ref name="GTRCONCEPTCARZ">{{cite web |url=http://www.conceptcarz.com/vehicle/z652/McLaren-F1-GTR.aspx |title=McLaren F1 GTR general information |publisher=ConceptCarz.com |access-date=19 August 2016}}</ref>

Some of the F1 GTRs, after the cars were no longer eligible for international racing series, were converted to street use. By adding mufflers, and passenger seats, adjusting the suspension for more ground clearance for public streets, and removing the air-restrictors, the cars were able to be registered for road use.

=== F1 GTR 1995 ===
[[File:1995 McLaren F1 GTR (20195044452).jpg|thumb|right|A 1995-spec F1 GTR, chassis #006R at the [[Goodwood Festival of Speed]] sporting the famous 'Harrods' livery]]

Built at the request of race teams, such as those owned by [[Ray Bellm]] and [[Thomas Bscher]], in order to compete in the [[BPR Global GT Series]], the [[McLaren F1 GTR]] was a custom-built race car which introduced a modified engine management system that increased power output – however, air-restrictors mandated by racing regulations reduced the power back to {{convert|441|kW|hp PS|0|abbr=on}} at 7,500&nbsp;rpm.<ref name="QV500GTR95">{{cite web |url=http://www.qv500.com/mclarenf1p5.php |title=McLaren F1 GTR 1995 general information |publisher=QV500 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060513141840/http://www.qv500.com/mclarenf1p5.php |archive-date=13 May 2006 |access-date=19 August 2016}}</ref> The car's list of modifications included changes to body panels, suspension, aerodynamics and the interior. The F1 GTR would go on to take its greatest achievement with first, third, fourth, fifth, and 13th places in the [[1995 24 Hours of Le Mans]], beating out custom built prototype sports cars. When [[Mark Blundell]] – who finished fourth in the race – was asked what the F1 GTR was like to drive during the wet race, he said: "Well it was never designed to be a race car so in many respects it wasn't the best-balanced car in the world. The saving grace of the car was the BMW V12 engine. It was incredibly impressive in that you could be in 6th gear at 2,000 rpm and the thing would just pull like a train. And in the wet that is great as you can run a higher gear and it cuts out some of that traction issue. But in terms of balance, overall it was always a bit top heavy, so the centre of gravity wasn’t ideal. And aerodynamically it wasn't quite there, but it did the job".<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.scalextric.com/uk-en/news/testtrack/scalextric-meets-mark-blundell|title=Scalextric Meets Mark Blundell|website=www.scalextric.com|access-date=2019-08-11}}</ref>

In total, nine F1 GTRs were built for the 1995 season.<ref name="QV500GTR95"/>

The 1995 F1 GTR created so much downforce that it was claimed to be able to drive upside down along a ceiling at {{convert|100|mph|km/h|0|abbr=on}}.<ref name=motortrend/>

=== F1 GTR 1996 ===
[[File:Brands Hatch-1996-09-08-006.jpg|left|thumb|A 1996-spec F1 GTR, chassis #015R at the [[Brands Hatch]] circuit]]

To follow up on the success of the F1 GTR in 1996, McLaren further developed the 1995 model, leading to a size increase but a weight decrease. Nine more F1 GTRs were built to 1996 spec, while some 1995 cars were still campaigned by privateers. F1 GTR 1996 chassis #14R is notable as being the first non-Japanese car to win a race in the [[Super GT|All-Japan Grand Touring Car Championship]] (JGTC).<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.motorsportretro.com/2014/01/jgtc-cars/ |title=The eight most iconic JGTC racing machines ever |publisher=MotorsportRetro.com |date=23 January 2014 |access-date=19 August 2016}}</ref> The car was driven by [[David Brabham]] and [[John Nielsen (racing driver)|John Nielsen]]. The weight was reduced by around {{convert|37|kg|abbr=on}} from the 1995 GTR but the engine was kept de-tuned at {{cvt|447|kW|hp PS|0|abbr=on}} to comply with racing regulations.<ref name="Autocar Road Test">{{cite web |url=http://forums.vwvortex.com/showthread.php?5356511-Archive-Autocar-Road-Test-of-McLaren-F1 |title=Archive: Autocar Road Test of McLaren F1 |date=8 July 2011 |access-date=19 August 2016}}</ref>

=== F1 GTR 1997 ===
[[File:Parabolica McLaren F1 GTR.jpg|thumb|right|A 1997-spec F1 GTR "Long Tail", chassis #027R of Parabolica Motorsports during an [[FIA GT Championship]] event]]

With three F1 GT homologation street versions produced, McLaren could now develop the F1 GTR for the 1997 season. Weight was further reduced and a [[sequential manual transmission|sequential gearbox]] was added. The engine was slightly destroked to 6.0&nbsp;L instead of the previous 6.1&nbsp;L. Due to the heavily modified bodywork, the F1 GTR 1997 is often referred to as the "Longtail" thanks to the rear bodywork being extended to increase downforce. A total of ten F1 GTRs were built for the 1997 season. The weight was reduced to a total of {{convert|910|kg||0|abbr=on}}.

